    "sections": {
      "copyright": {
        "title": "Copyright — classical emaki are in the public domain",
        "body": "Most emaki on this site were created between the Heian and Edo periods, and their creators died centuries ago. In Japan, copyright generally expires 70 years after the author's death, so the original works of these scrolls are in the public domain.",
        "points": [
          "However, the important point is that the work itself and the image are different things. The conditions for using a photographed or digitized image are governed by the license set by the institution that holds the original (museum, library, or digital archive).",
          "In other words, \"the work is public domain, therefore the image is free to use\" is not necessarily true. Please check the license of the institution that is the source of the image.",
          "This site displays the source and processing information (resolution adjustment, WebP conversion, etc.) on each scroll page. When using an image, follow that source information."
        ],
        "official": {
          "title": "Official pages",
